    "q-83404": {
      "dom_id": "step-q83404",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨\\frac{2{t}^{3}}{3},{t}^{2},\\,\\,t⟩$ for $t>0$, use the cross product curvature formula to compute the curvature at the point $P(\\frac{2}{3},1,1).$",
      "worked_solution": "The curvature $κ(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to the point $P(\\frac{2}{3},1,1)$ on the given curve. We know that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨\\frac{2}{3},1,1⟩ \\\\ ⟨\\frac{2{t}^{3}}{3},{t}^{2},\\,\\,t⟩ & =⟨\\frac{2}{3},1,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the respective components gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2{t}^{3}}{3}=\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ {t}^{2}=1 \\\\ t=1.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equality, $t=1.$ This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(\\frac{2}{3},1,1)$ is at the tip of $r(1).$ \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$, ${r}^{′}(1),$  and $‖{r}^{′}(1)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2{t}^{3}}{3}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2{t}^{2},\\,2t,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(1) & =⟨2,2,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(1)‖ & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find ${r}^{″}(t)$ and ${r}^{″}(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4t,\\,2,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(1) & =⟨4,2,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross-product and its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(1)\\times {r}^{″}(1) & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =-2i+4j-4k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(1)\\times {r}^{″}(1)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{4}^{2}+(-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(1) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(1)\\times {r}^{″}(1)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(1){‖}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{{3}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
