    "q-83407": {
      "dom_id": "step-q83407",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨{e}^{t},\\,{e}^{2t},\\,2t⟩,$ use the cross product curvature formula to compute the curvature at the point $P(1,1,0).$",
      "worked_solution": "The curvature $κ(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to the point $P(1,1,0)$ on the given curve. We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨1,1,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨{e}^{t},\\,{e}^{2t},\\,2t⟩ & =⟨1,1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the respective components gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t}=1 \\\\ {e}^{2t}=1 \\\\ 2t=0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equality, $t=0.$ This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(1,1,0)$ is at the tip of $r(0).$ \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$, ${r}^{′}(0),$  and $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{2t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t},\\,2{e}^{2t},\\,2⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(0) & =⟨1,2,2⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find ${r}^{″}(t)$ and ${r}^{″}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{e}^{2t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t},\\,4{e}^{2t},\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(0) & =⟨1,4,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross-product and its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0) & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =-8i+2j+2k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-8)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(0) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(0){‖}^{3}}=\\frac{6\\sqrt{2}}{{3}^{3}}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-83407-a",
