    "q-131959": {
      "dom_id": "step-q131959",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4t⟩$ for $0\\le t<2\\pi ,$ use the cross product curvature formula to compute the curvature at the point $P(0,-3,4\\pi ).$",
      "worked_solution": "The curvature $κ(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to the point $P(0,-3,4\\pi )$ on the given curve. We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨0,-3,4\\pi ⟩ \\\\ ⟨3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4t⟩ & =⟨0,-3,4\\pi ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the respective components gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=0 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=-3 \\\\ 4t=4\\pi .\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equality, $t=\\pi .$ This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(0,-3,4\\pi )$ is at the tip of $r(\\pi ).$ \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$, ${r}^{′}(\\pi ),$  and $‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,4⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(\\pi ) & =⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ,-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ,4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3,0,4⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖ & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{0}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find ${r}^{″}(t)$ and ${r}^{″}(\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(\\pi ) & =⟨-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ,-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,3,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross-product and its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(\\pi )\\times {r}^{″}(\\pi ) & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -3 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =-12i-9k \\\\  & =3(-4i-3k) \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )\\times {r}^{″}(\\pi )‖ & =3\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{16+9} \\\\  & =3(5) \\\\  & =15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=\\pi $ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(\\pi ) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )\\times {r}^{″}(\\pi )‖}{‖{r}^{′}(\\pi ){‖}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{{5}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
