      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨6-3{t}^{2},\\,4,\\,6t-2⟩$, use the cross product curvature formula to find the curvature as a function of $t.$",
      "worked_solution": "The curvature $κ(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6-3{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6t-2)⟩=⟨-6t,\\,0,\\,6⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-6t)^{2}+{0}^{2}+(6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36{t}^{2}+36} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36({t}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the second derivative ${r}^{″}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-6t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6)⟩=⟨-6,\\,0,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the cross-product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t) & =⟨-6t,0,6⟩\\times ⟨-6,0,0⟩ \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -6t & 0 & 6 \\\\ -6 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}-6t & 6 \\\\ -6 & 0\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}-6t & 0 \\\\ -6 & 0\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =-36\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the magnitude of the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖ & =36\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}=\\frac{36}{{(6\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1})}^{3}}=\\frac{1}{6({t}^{2}+1)^{3/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
