    "q-165241": {
      "dom_id": "step-q165241",
      "name": "Question 10",
      "problem": "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,t-2⟩,$ use the cross product curvature formula to find the curvature of this curve as a function of $t.$ \n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that ${r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+4k.$*",
      "worked_solution": "The curvature $κ(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡t+4{cos}^{2}⁡t+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we are told that\n\n$$\n{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+4k,\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{(-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(4)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+16} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{(\\sqrt{5})^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
