    "q-132067": {
      "dom_id": "step-q132067",
      "name": "Question 11",
      "problem": "Using the cross product curvature formula, find the curvature of $r(t)=⟨t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩$ as a function of $t.$",
      "worked_solution": "The curvature $κ$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡t-2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{t}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t+2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{t}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{t}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)+({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(t)$ and ${r}^{″}(t)$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,0⟩\\times ⟨-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & \\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & 0 \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & \\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =[(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)]k \\\\  & =[2{cos}^{2}⁡t-3t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{t}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡t+2{sin}^{2}⁡t+3t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{t}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡t]k \\\\  & =(2({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)+{t}^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t))k \\\\  & =({t}^{2}+2)k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the magnitude of the cross product is\n\n$$\n‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖={t}^{2}+2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}+2}{{(\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1})}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}+2}{({t}^{2}+1)^{3/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
